Third-party font

kernel/drivers/font_ttf.c and font_ttf.h contain anti-aliased glyph
bitmaps baked from JetBrains Mono (Regular), which is licensed under
the SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1, not the MIT License above.
The full OFL text is included at tools/OFL.txt and must accompany any
redistribution of that font data (or substantial rendered derivatives
of it), per the OFL's own terms.

data/fonts/ contains five complete third-party font files, shipped
verbatim onto the disk image as /usr/share/fonts and loaded at runtime
(see kernel/drivers/font_face.c). None is covered by the MIT License
above:

  dejavu-sans-mono.ttf       DejaVu Sans Mono, under the Bitstream
  dejavu-sans-mono-bold.ttf  Vera Fonts Copyright and the Arev Fonts
                             Copyright. Full text:
                             data/fonts/LICENSE-DejaVu.txt.

  liberation-sans.ttf        Liberation Sans, under the SIL Open Font
  liberation-sans-bold.ttf   License, Version 1.1. Full text:
                             data/fonts/LICENSE-Liberation.txt.

  vera-mono.ttf              Bitstream Vera Sans Mono, under the
                             Bitstream Vera Fonts Copyright. Full
                             text: data/fonts/LICENSE-Vera.txt.

All three license files are copied onto the disk image alongside the
fonts themselves, because each license requires the notice to travel
with the font.

---

Third-party code

This repository vendors four third-party programs. None is covered by
the MIT License above, and each keeps its own license file in its own
directory.

  userland/ports/doom/    doomgeneric, a portable Doom source port
                          derived from id Software's Doom, under the
                          GNU General Public License, version 2 OR (at
                          your option) any later version. Full text:
                          userland/ports/doom/LICENSE.

                          The music files in that directory
                          (i_oplmusic.c, midifile.c/.h, mus2mid.c and
                          opl/) come from Chocolate Doom
                          (chocolate-doom-2.1.0), which doomgeneric is
                          itself derived from, under the same licence.
                          opl/dbopl.c is DOSBox's OPL emulator, also
                          GPL-2.0-or-later. See that directory's
                          README.md.

        WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE. The GPL is a license on
        DISTRIBUTION, and this directory is an aggregation: it builds
        into one binary (/bin/doom) that nothing else in the system
        links against, which the build enforces through
        EXTRA_OBJS_doom. The MIT-licensed toy-os code that binary
        links (tolibc, the Toykit) is GPL-compatible, so the resulting
        /bin/doom is distributable under the GPL; the rest of this
        repository is unaffected and remains MIT. Anyone
        redistributing a built /bin/doom must offer the corresponding
        source for it, which this repository is.

        The Doom IWAD is NOT part of this repository and is not
        redistributed by it -- tools/fetch_wad.py obtains one, and the
        WAD carries id Software's own terms, which are not an open
        source license.

  userland/ports/cjson/   cJSON, Copyright (c) 2009-2017 Dave Gamble
                          and cJSON contributors, under the MIT
                          License. Full text:
                          userland/ports/cjson/LICENSE. Its copyright
                          notice is separate from toy-os's above and
                          must be preserved.

  userland/ports/mbedtls/ Mbed TLS, Copyright The Mbed TLS Contributors,
                          dual licensed Apache License 2.0 OR (at your
                          option) the GNU General Public License,
                          version 2 or later. Full text:
                          userland/ports/mbedtls/LICENSE.

        TOY-OS TAKES THE APACHE-2.0 OPTION, which the dual licence
        explicitly permits the recipient to choose. Apache-2.0 is a
        permissive licence and aggregates with the MIT code above
        without reaching any of it, so -- unlike the Doom port -- there
        is no reason to confine it to a single binary, and it is built
        as a shared library (/lib/libssl.so) that several programs
        link. Its copyright notices and the NOTICE-style attribution in
        each file are separate from toy-os's and must be preserved.

        WHAT IS NOT VENDORED. The Mozilla CA certificate bundle is not
        in this repository. `make iso EXTRAS=1` fetches it, shows its
        licence (MPL-2.0) before doing so, and records the acceptance;
        a default build ships an empty trust store. Fetching is not
        distributing -- an image built with EXTRAS=1 carries
        /usr/share/licenses/extras.txt saying what is inside it.

  userland/ports/dash/    dash, the Debian Almquist Shell, Copyright
                          (c) 1989-1994 The Regents of the University
                          of California, (c) 1997 Christos Zoulas and
                          (c) 1997-2005 Herbert Xu, under the BSD
                          3-Clause License. Full text:
                          userland/ports/dash/COPYING.

        ONE FILE IN IT IS NOT BSD. src/mksignames.c is Copyright (C)
        1992 Free Software Foundation, Inc., taken from GNU Bash, under
        the GNU General Public License, version 2 or (at your option)
        any later version. dash's own COPYING carries that notice as a
        second section and states the consequence: the file is not
        linked into the shell, but its output -- the generated
        signames.c signal-name table -- is. Nothing in this repository
        builds dash yet, so today it is vendored source and no more
        than that; a binary built from it as it stands would have to be
        distributed under the GPL, exactly as /bin/doom is. See that
        **IT IS NOT COMPILED, AND THEREFORE NOT DISTRIBUTED IN ANY
        BINARY.** The build generates that table with
        tools/gen_signames.py instead, from this kernel's own signal
        numbers -- which it had to do regardless of the licence, since
        mksignames.c reads the HOST's <signal.h> and would have put
        Linux's signal set in a toy-os shell. /bin/dash contains no GPL
        code. The file stays in the vendored tree because the port is
        kept byte for byte and deleting it would be a divergence to
        carry forever; it is listed here so a reader of this file can
        learn it is present.

